                <pb facs="0634" n="526" sameAs="frus:frus1893/pg526"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Foster to Mr. Wurts.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1892-08-23"><hi rendition="#italic">August 23,
                        1892</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 212.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I inclose herewith a statement with
                    accompanying certificates made to this Department by Mrs. Jennie Goldstein, of
                    New York City, concerning the alleged arrest and imprisonment of her husband,
                    Jacob Goldstein, at Kharkov, Russia, on the ground that he “is amenable to
                    militia duties.”</p>
                <p>The records of the Department confirm this statement of Messrs. C. B. Richard
                    &amp; Co., that Mr. Goldstein received a passport, No. 35320, from this
                    Department. It was issued February 23, 1892, and sent to Messrs. Richard &amp;
                    Co., to be forwarded. From Mr. Goldstein’s application it appears that he was
                    born at Tszelecz, in Russia, July 4, 1862; emigrated to this country in 1879,
                    and was naturalized before the superior court of the city of New York, October
                    8, 1888; his certificate of naturalization having been duly produced with his
                    application for a passport.</p>
                <p>I will thank you to apply in the proper quarter for information concerning the
                    reported arrest of Mr. Goldstein and the nature of the charges against him.
                    Should his case fall within the purview of the standing instructions of your
                    legation, you will take such action as may be necessary and proper to protect
                    Mr. Goldstein’s interest.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John W. Foster.</hi></signed></closer>





